NNAAMMEE
     wwaattcchhddooggdd — watchdog daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     wwaattcchhddooggdd [--ddnnSSww] [----ddeebbuugg] [----ssooffttttiimmeeoouutt] [----ssooffttttiimmeeoouutt--aaccttiioonn _a_c_t_i_o_n]
               [----pprreettiimmeeoouutt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t] [----pprreettiimmeeoouutt--aaccttiioonn _a_c_t_i_o_n] [--ee _c_m_d]
               [--II _f_i_l_e] [--ss _s_l_e_e_p] [--tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t] [--TT _s_c_r_i_p_t___t_i_m_e_o_u_t]
               [--xx _e_x_i_t___t_i_m_e_o_u_t]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The wwaattcchhddooggdd utility interfaces with the kernel's watchdog facility to
     ensure that the system is in a working state.  If wwaattcchhddooggdd is unable to
     interface with the kernel over a specific timeout, the kernel will take
     actions to assist in debugging or restarting the computer.

     If --ee _c_m_d is specified, wwaattcchhddooggdd will attempt to execute this command
     with system(3), and only if the command returns with a zero exit code
     will the watchdog be reset.  If --ee _c_m_d is not specified, the daemon will
     perform a trivial file system check instead.

     The --nn argument 'dry-run' will cause watchdog not to arm the system
     watchdog and instead only run the watchdog function and report on fail‐
     ures.  This is useful for developing new watchdogd scripts as the system
     will not reboot if there are problems with the script.

     The --ss _s_l_e_e_p argument can be used to control the sleep period between
     each execution of the check and defaults to 10 seconds.

     The --tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t specifies the desired timeout period in seconds.  The de‐
     fault timeout is 128 seconds.

     One possible circumstance which will cause a watchdog timeout is an in‐
     terrupt storm.  If this occurs, wwaattcchhddooggdd will no longer execute and thus
     the kernel's watchdog routines will take action after a configurable
     timeout.

     The --TT _s_c_r_i_p_t___t_i_m_e_o_u_t specifies the threshold (in seconds) at which the
     watchdogd will complain that its script has run for too long.  If unset
     _s_c_r_i_p_t___t_i_m_e_o_u_t defaults to the value specified by the --ss _s_l_e_e_p option.

     The --xx _e_x_i_t___t_i_m_e_o_u_t argument is the timeout period (in seconds) to leave
     in effect when the program exits.  Using --xx with a non-zero value pro‐
     tects against lockup during a reboot by triggering a hardware reset if
     the software reboot doesn't complete before the given timeout expires.

     Upon receiving the SIGTERM or SIGINT signals, wwaattcchhddooggdd will terminate,
     after first instructing the kernel to either disable the timeout or reset
     it to the value given by --xx _e_x_i_t___t_i_m_e_o_u_t.

     The wwaattcchhddooggdd utility recognizes the following runtime options:

     --II _f_i_l_e                         Write the process ID of the wwaattcchhddooggdd
                                     utility in the specified file.

     --dd ----ddeebbuugg                      Do not fork.  When this option is speci‐
                                     fied, wwaattcchhddooggdd will not fork into the
                                     background at startup.

     --SS                              Do not send a message to the system log‐
                                     ger when the watchdog command takes
                                     longer than expected to execute.  The de‐
                                     fault behaviour is to log a warning via
                                     the system logger with the LOG_DAEMON fa‐
                                     cility, and to output a warning to stan‐
                                     dard error.

     --ww                              Complain when the watchdog script takes
                                     too long.  This flag will cause watchdogd
                                     to complain when the amount of time to
                                     execute the watchdog script exceeds the
                                     threshold of 'sleep' option.

     ----pprreettiimmeeoouutt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t            Set a "pretimeout" watchdog.  At "time‐
                                     out" seconds before the watchdog will
                                     fire attempt an action.  The action is
                                     set by the --pretimeout-action flag.  The
                                     default is just to log a message
                                     (WD_SOFT_LOG) via log(9).

     ----pprreettiimmeeoouutt--aaccttiioonn _a_c_t_i_o_n      Set the timeout action for the pretime‐
                                     out.  See the section _T_i_m_e_o_u_t _A_c_t_i_o_n_s.

     ----ssooffttttiimmeeoouutt                   Instead of arming the various hardware
                                     watchdogs, only use a basic software
                                     watchdog.  The default action is just to
                                     log(9) a message (WD_SOFT_LOG).

     ----ssooffttttiimmeeoouutt--aaccttiioonn _a_c_t_i_o_n     Set the timeout action for the softtime‐
                                     out.  See the section _T_i_m_e_o_u_t _A_c_t_i_o_n_s.

TTiimmeeoouutt AAccttiioonnss
     The following timeout actions are available via the ----pprreettiimmeeoouutt--aaccttiioonn
     and ----ssooffttttiimmeeoouutt--aaccttiioonn flags:

     _p_a_n_i_c   Call panic(9) when the timeout is reached.

     _d_d_b     Enter the kernel debugger via kdb_enter(9) when the timeout is
             reached.

     _l_o_g     Log a message using log(9) when the timeout is reached.

     _p_r_i_n_t_f  call the kernel printf(9) to display a message to the console and
             dmesg(8) buffer.

     Actions can be combined in a comma separated list as so: _l_o_g_,_p_r_i_n_t_f which
     would both printf(9) and log(9) which will send messages both to dmesg(8)
     and the kernel log(4) device for syslogd(8).

